The '''History of Sarta (1977–1998)''' covers the period between the end of the PUM-dominated postwar consensus and the Labor Party's return to government in 1998. The first half was marked by stagnation, industrial decline, and unemployment after the [[1976–1981 global economic crisis]]. A conservative victory in 1984 then produced a major policy shift toward privatization and services. Rapid growth followed between 1990 and 1994, but the expansion weakened after 1995 and ended with renewed unemployment and a change of government in 1998.

== 1977–1984: stagnation and economic crisis ==

The global recession that followed the Camboriú War and the oil shock reduced demand for manufactured goods across the region. Sarta was particularly exposed because its postwar growth had depended on exports to [[Volisania]] and [[Jacan]], as well as on manufacturing relocated from Volisania during the preceding decades. As foreign demand contracted, factories reduced production, postponed investment, and shed workers.

The [[1976–1981 global economic crisis]] also disrupted the international dollar system and raised the cost of energy and credit. Sartan manufacturers faced weaker orders at the same time that transport and production became more expensive. The country entered a prolonged period of stagnation. Population continued to rise, but employment and household incomes did not keep pace, producing visible unemployment in industrial districts and the larger ports.

The government initially resisted privatization. It retained state ownership of major industries and attempted to wait out the downturn, arguing that strategic enterprises should not be sold during a temporary international crisis. This limited the immediate loss of public control but prolonged the financial burden on the state and delayed industrial restructuring.

== 1984–1990: conservative reform and privatization ==

A new conservative party won the 1984 election on a programme of economic reform. Its leaders argued that the PUM-era model had become too dependent on protected industries and public ownership, and that recovery required private investment, greater competition, and a shift away from heavy manufacturing.

The new government introduced a broad privatization programme. State enterprises were sold or reorganised, and private firms were given a larger role in transport, communications, finance, utilities, and industrial production. The process was controversial, particularly in areas where public companies had been major employers, but it brought new capital into sectors that had suffered from years of underinvestment.

Economic policy also shifted toward services. Banking, insurance, logistics, retail, tourism, business administration, and communications expanded as manufacturing employment declined. The government presented this as a transition to a more flexible economy suited to international trade. In many industrial towns, however, the change was experienced as a loss of stable employment before replacement service work became available.

== 1990–1994: rapid growth ==

The reforms coincided with an improvement in regional demand and a revival of international investment. Sarta's service industries grew quickly, while newer manufacturing operations supplied regional markets with lighter and more specialised goods. Privatized firms increased productivity and expanded into areas that had previously been managed through state planning.

From 1990 to 1994, Sarta recorded annual GDP growth in the double digits. The expansion was visible in rising employment, construction, household consumption, and urban development. Financial and commercial districts grew around the main cities, and service-sector work became an increasingly important source of employment for younger and more educated workers.

The growth did not eliminate the structural limits of the Sartan economy. The country still relied on external markets, imported energy, and foreign investment. Manufacturing was more productive than before but no longer employed as large a share of the population, and many regions benefited less from the boom than the major urban centres. Nevertheless, the first half of the 1990s was remembered as a period of exceptional prosperity after the stagnation of the previous decade.

== 1995–1998: renewed unemployment and political change ==

Growth slowed after 1995 as external demand weakened and the initial effects of privatization faded. Some firms that had expanded rapidly during the boom reduced their workforces, while older industrial districts continued to lose manufacturing employment. Service-sector growth remained positive but was insufficient to absorb all displaced workers and new entrants to the labour market.

Unemployment rose again, becoming the central political issue of the late 1990s. Critics of the conservative government argued that privatization had transferred public assets without creating secure employment, while supporters maintained that the reforms had modernised an economy that would otherwise have declined further. The disagreement divided cities and industrial regions from the newer commercial centres that had benefited most from the expansion.

The Labor Party won the 1998 election, ending the conservative party's period in power. Its campaign promised to preserve the service economy while strengthening employment protections, retraining programmes, and public investment. The election marked the end of the 1977–1998 era and the beginning of a new phase in which the political debate centred on how to distribute the gains and costs of liberalized growth. The following period developed into a recurring alternation between Labor and conservative governments rather than a return to the single-party dominance of the PUM era.

